{
 "family": "tasks",
 "note": "Ruling Q1: one function of internal/admin, TaskRefusal, turns an error of internal/tasks into a refusal. It finds a refusal of the scheduler with errors.Is, also when the error wraps it: an unknown task is not-found (404), a disabled task task-disabled (405), a running task task-running (409), a task that is not running task-not-running (409), and a scheduler that has not started or is closed unavailable (503). nil stays nil, and every other error passes unchanged (key passes), so that Answer gives 500 for it; the refusals ErrDuplicate and ErrStarted of the scheduler are such errors. A case of op error names the error (key error) and how it is wrapped (key wrap): empty for not at all, fmt as the scheduler wraps its refusals, twice for a second fmt.Errorf around that, join for errors.Join with another error before it. The key task is the id the caller passes; the messages of the four task codes name it. Ruling Q2: the same function turns a *tasks.FieldError into a refusal with the code schedule-invalid, 400 at schedule.<Field>, with the Message of the error. A case of op schedule checks its schedule with tasks.Schedule.Trigger and passes the error, wrapped as the key wrap says, to TaskRefusal. The five fields are type, startAt, weekDays, monthDays and cron; startAt is an instant in RFC 3339. Ruling Q3 (op settings): a create or save of a task, a body of the shape TaskSettings of the contract (S40), is checked by CheckTask against the types below. typeId names one of them, else 400 at typeId (task-type); a save whose typeId differs from the stored one (key stored) is refused with 400 at typeId (task-type-changed), an empty typeId among them; the name is not empty after trimming white space and has at most 256 characters, else 400 at name (task-name); the schedule is checked by tasks.Schedule.Trigger and refused as Q2 says; the refusals of one request travel together, in the order typeId, name, enabled, schedule, properties, the order of the fields of the contract. A key nameRepeat repeats the name of the body that many times; a character is a Unicode code point, so 256 times a counts as 256. The 256 characters count the name after trimming white space, as the emptiness check does (ruling Q3), so white space around a name of 256 characters passes, and white space inside a name counts. The properties are checked against the type of typeId when the file holds that type and typeId is not refused. Ruling Q4 (op properties): CheckTaskProperties checks the properties of a body (key properties) against the type named by the key type. Every value is a string, else 400 at properties.<id> (property-value), null and a number among them; a required field is present and not empty, else property-missing; a value that is not empty fits its field: a number is a whole number in decimal digits with a minus sign before a negative one that fits in a signed 64 bit integer (ruling Q4a, rows Q4a) and has no plus sign (ruling Q4b, rows Q4b); a boolean is true or false; a select is one of the values of its options; a repository is * or a repository of the snapshot below; a blobstore is a blob store of the snapshot below. An empty value of a field that is not required is no value and passes, for every field type (ruling Q4c, rows Q4c, beside an empty value of a required field). A property the type does not define is refused with property-unknown. A field of a type outside the six words of the contract refuses every value that is not empty (ruling Q4d, rows Q4d, the type demo.odd). The refusals come in the order of the fields of the type and then of the other ids, sorted. The general rule R6 holds for Q3 and Q4: a save never refuses a value the stored task holds, its typeId, its name and its schedule (key stored), and a property whose value equals the stored one (key storedProperties), whatever its name. The stored typeId demo.retired names a type this server does not have, as an import may bring one; a save that keeps it is not refused and its properties are not checked. On a save, a required property absent from the body and from the stored task is refused, because R6 covers stored values only (ruling Q4e, rows Q4e). Ruling Q16: a select field without options, as the type demo.free has, takes any string value that is not empty; a required one still refuses an empty or absent value, and a value that is no string is refused as for every field. Ruling Q17: a body of op settings may hold enabled (contract S40). When it holds the key, the value is a JSON boolean, else 400 at enabled with the code task-enabled; a string such as yes or false, null and a number are refused, never read as false. The refusal comes after the one of the name and before the ones of the schedule, the order of the fields of the contract. The key enabledAfter of a case is the value TaskEnabled gives for an accepted body: absent on a create means true, absent on a save keeps the stored value, and a value of the body wins. The types, repositories and blob stores are invented.",
 "types": [
